    <h1>Alexa Skill Certification Privacy Review: What Amazon Checks Before Your Skill Goes Live</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published October 31, 2026 &middot; Checked against Amazon's Alexa developer documentation on that date</p>

    <p>Alexa skill certification reviews privacy in four places. Reviewers open your <strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong> and apply Amazon's three privacy-URL tests. They check the <strong>Privacy requirements</strong> and <strong>Skills that use account linking</strong> sections of the Security Requirements. They compare your answers on the <strong>Privacy &amp; Compliance</strong> page with what the skill actually does. And if the skill targets children, they apply section 2 of the Policy Requirements, <strong>Child-directed Alexa skills</strong>. A policy URL is mandatory when a skill uses account linking or collects personal information, whether through voice or through skill permissions.</p>

    <p>This page covers the review itself. For the policy document, an example and hosting, see the <a href="/resources/alexa-skill-privacy-policy/">Alexa skill privacy policy guide and example</a>. This page is not legal advice.</p>

    <nav class="toc">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#what-certification-checks">What Certification Checks on Privacy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rejection-reasons">Why Skills Get Rejected on Privacy, and How to Pre-empt Each Reason</a></li>
        <li><a href="#policy-url-console">Where the Policy URL Goes and What Makes It Fail</a></li>
